.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_ENABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_enable_sending \- turn on sends on a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_enable_sending(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Clears the flag that ccol_circq_disable_sending() set, so the queue
accepts sends again, and wakes every thread that is blocked waiting to
send. A woken sender goes on as soon as the queue has room for its
message.

A new queue starts with sends on, so you only need this call to reopen
a queue whose sends were turned off. Calling it on a queue whose sends
are already on is harmless.

It also wakes every write-direction waiter on the queue (a ccol_select()
call, a ccol_select_timed() call or a ccol_event_loop registration), and
each of them scans again and sees the new state. While sends are off
the queue is ready for the write direction whatever its message count
(see ccol_circq_disable_sending(3)), but from this call on a full queue
is not ready for writing until a receive frees a slot.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue that gets sends turned on
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - Sends are on again
ccol_invalid_args  - cq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Blocked senders are woken with a broadcast, so a sender can try again
at once if the queue has free space. Receives are not affected, and the
function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_circq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_timed_send_zc (3)
